/* Língua Inglesa — 8º ano, prática gradual de leitura e gramática. */
window.portal8AppendCurriculum?.("Inglês","Simple past|Choose the correct past tense of \"go\".|Went|Goed|Going|Goes|Go is irregular: its simple past form is went.\nSimple past|Complete: \"Yesterday, she ___ a book.\"|read|reads|reading|will read|Read has the same spelling in present and past, though the pronunciation changes.\nSimple past|Which sentence describes a finished event in the past?|They visited the museum last Sunday.|They visit the museum every Sunday.|They will visit tomorrow.|They are visiting now.|Last Sunday indicates a completed past event.\nPast continuous|Complete: \"I ___ watching TV when you called.\"|was|were|am|be|The subject I combines with was in the past continuous.\nPast continuous|Complete: \"They ___ studying at 8 p.m. yesterday.\"|were|was|are|is|They were studying expresses an action in progress in the past.\nPast continuous|Which form combines with \"was\" to describe an ongoing past action?|Verb ending in -ing|Base verb only|Infinitive with to always|Past participle only|Past continuous follows was/were + verb-ing.\nFuture with going to|Complete: \"We are going to ___ tomorrow.\"|travel|traveled|travels|traveling always|Be going to + base verb expresses a plan or prediction.\nFuture with will|Complete: \"I think it ___ rain tomorrow.\"|will|was|were|had|Will + base verb is used for future predictions.\nComparatives|What is the comparative of \"big\"?|Bigger|Biggest|More bigest|Biger|For short adjectives ending in consonant-vowel-consonant, the final consonant often doubles.\nComparatives|Complete: \"A bus is usually ___ than a bicycle.\"|heavier|heaviest|heavy|more heavyest|Heavier is the comparative of heavy.\nSuperlatives|What is the superlative of \"good\"?|Best|Goodest|More good|Better than|Good has irregular comparative better and superlative best.\nSuperlatives|Complete: \"This is the ___ day of the week for me.\"|best|better|gooder|more well|The superlative best marks the highest degree.\nModal verbs|Which modal expresses ability?|Can|Mustn't always|Shouldn't always|Mightn't only|Can is often used to express ability.\nModal verbs|Complete: \"You ___ wear a seat belt in a car.\"|must|might not necessarily|was|had been|Must expresses obligation in this statement.\nAdvice|Which sentence gives advice?|You should study regularly.|You studied yesterday.|They are studying now.|I will go tomorrow.|Should is commonly used to give advice.\nConditionals|Complete: \"If it rains, we ___ stay indoors.\"|will|was|were|having|First conditional often uses if + simple present and will + base verb.\nConnectors|Which connector signals contrast?|However|Because|Therefore|Moreover|However contrasts ideas.\nConnectors|Which connector expresses a result?|Therefore|Although|Despite|While|Therefore usually introduces a consequence or result.\nVocabulary|What does \"environment\" mean in Portuguese?|Meio ambiente|Entretenimento|Encontro|Experiência|Environment refers to the natural or surrounding environment.\nVocabulary|What is the opposite of \"dangerous\"?|Safe|Risky|Hazardous|Unsafe|Safe means not dangerous.\nVocabulary|What does \"borrow\" mean?|Pegar emprestado|Emprestar a alguém|Comprar|Vender|Borrow is receiving something temporarily; lend is giving it temporarily.\nReading comprehension|In \"Maya studies every morning\", when does Maya study?|Every morning|Only on weekends|Every night|Never|Every morning means todas as manhãs.\nReading comprehension|In \"The museum is closed on Mondays\", when is it not open?|On Mondays|On Fridays|On Saturdays|Every evening|Closed on Mondays indicates the museum does not open that day.\nPronouns|Choose the correct possessive adjective: \"This is ___ backpack.\" (she)|her|his|their|our|Her is the possessive adjective referring to she.\nPronouns|Complete: \"My friends and I prepared ___ presentation.\"|our|his|her|its|Our refers to the speaker and at least one other person.\nCultural diversity|Which is a respectful way to learn about English-speaking cultures?|Explore different voices and contexts without stereotypes|Assume all speak exactly the same|Ignore cultural differences|Judge accents as inferior|English is used across countries and communities with varied cultures.\nDigital literacy|Before sharing an English-language article online, it is useful to:|Check the source and date|Share immediately without reading|Trust only the headline|Ignore the author|Critical reading of digital media applies across languages.\nQuestions|Which question correctly asks about a past activity?|What did you do yesterday?|What you did yesterday?|What did you yesterday doed?|What does you did?|Questions in simple past often use did + subject + base verb.\nFrequency adverbs|Which adverb means \"sempre\"?|Always|Never|Sometimes|Rarely|Always indicates that something happens on every relevant occasion.\n");
